Spintronics, also known as spin transport electronics, is mainly focused on the study of the intrinsic spin and magnetic moment of the electrons, and has shown great potential in quantum computing and data storage.[1] As the prototypical organic spintronic devices, organic spin valves (OSVs) have gained intensive interest since their discovery in 2004.[2] OSVs mainly comprise two ferromagnetic (FM) electrodes with different coercive fields and a sandwiched nonmagnetic (NM) organic spacer layer.[3] It is well accepted that the MR magnitudes of OSVs depend sensitively on the spin-polarized electronic nature of the interface between FM electrodes and organic spacer (i.e. spinterface).[4] Theoretical results demonstrate that organic semiconductors usually exert negligible influence on the spatial spin polarization of the bottom metal oxide FM electrode[5a] but greatly affect the spinterface with the top metallic FM electrodes (e.g. Co) and even cause the spin polarization inversion.[5b-c] Thus, the interface engineering in OSVs is vital to their final performance. Although a recently developed top-electrodes transfer technique[6] may provide an effective way to build a damage-free spinterface, it is not adaptable for future industrial process. Conventional organic spacers in OSVs are usually small molecules[2,7] or linear π-conjugated polymers[8] with relatively poor performance. It is highly desirable to explore novel semiconductors for OSVs that could intrinsically form proper spinterfaces with the top metal FM electrodes and without frustrating their spin polarization.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are crystalline porous coordination polymers in which metal ions or inorganic clusters are connected by organic linkers.[9] Most reported MOFs exhibit low conductivity or even are insulators.[10a] Loading or doping of non-innocent guests in MOFs could realize enhanced electrical conductivity.[10b-c] Very recently, 2D $c$ -MOFs have emerged as a fascinating platform for various electronic applications such as chemiresistive sensors,[10d-f] thermoelectrics,[11] superconductors,[12] ferromagnetic semiconductors,[13] and battery electrodes,[14] etc. The most established 2D $c$ -MOFs are realized by integration of symmetric redox ligands containing multitropic ortho-substituted -OH, -NH2 or -SH functional groups attached to planar conjugated aromatic cores and late transition metal ions in a square planar coordination geometry.[15,16] 2D cMOFs feature extended 2D $\pi$ -conjugation and layered structures that are reminiscent of graphene, and thus exhibit high conductivity (up to $2500\mathsf{S c m}^{-1})^{[17]}$ and large charge mobility (up to $220\mathsf{c m}^{2}\mathsf{V}^{-1}\mathsf{s}^{-1};$ .[18] We envision that the combination of 2D cMOFs and OSVs could not only build a well-defined spinterface with the metal electrodes but also evoke rich physics, flexible chemistry, and potential applications in new generation OSVs. However, to the best of our knowledge, 2D $c$ -MOF based OSVs have never been reported, and the possible magnetic field modulation effect on the spin transport in such materials remains unexplored.
As a proof of concept, we selected one of the most available and well established 2D $c$ -MOF, $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ ,[19] as the organic NM layer for the fabrication of OSVs. Due to the insolubility of the large extended 2D skeleton, traditional film processing techniques such as spin-coating or drop-casting common for soluble linear polymer-based OSVs were not adequate. Thus, an optimized layer-by-layer assembled method was applied for the preparation of 2D $c$ -MOF thin film on FM electrodes.[20] The spin injection and transport of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ films was investigated in OSVs with a large negative MR of $25\%$ at $10~\mathsf{K}.$ Moreover, a distinctive MR effect could even be retained at high temperature up to $200\mathsf{K}.$
Half-metallic $\mathsf{L a}_{0.67}\mathsf{S r}_{0.33}\mathsf{M n O}_{3}$ (LSMO), known as a stable ferromagnet with near $100\%$ spin-polarization at low temperatures,[21] was chosen as the bottom FM electrodes, and $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ film was applied as the spacer layer. The $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ films were in-situ prepared on the LSMO electrodes via layer-by-layer growth (Figure 1a).[20,22] To better control the interface between the bottom LSMO electrodes and 2D $\scriptstyle{c}$ -MOF films and further enhance the organic/electrode contact, a selfassembled monolayer (SAM) of 3-aminopropyltrimethoxysilane (3-APTMS) was covalently anchored on the LSMO surface.[10b,23] The detailed procedure was summarized in the supporting information (Section 1). The growth of the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ film on
quartz glass was monitored by UV-vis spectra measurement (Figures 2b and S1). The characteristic absorbance at $363\mathsf{n m}$ (π- $\pi^{\star}$ transition) and $648\mathsf{n m}$ (ligand to metal charge transfer band)[24] intensified with increasing growth cycles. The thickness of the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ film was proportional to the number of cycles (Figures 2c and S2), at a growth rate of about 5 nm per cycle. Atomic force microscopy (AFM) and scanning electronic microscopy (SEM) images confirm the continuity and good coverage of the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ film (Figures S2 and S3).
To characterize the crystallinity and orientation of the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ film, grazing-incidence $\mathsf{X}$ -ray diffraction (GIXRD) measurements with both in-plane and out-of-plane scattering geometry were performed. Three prominent diffraction peaks at $2\theta=4.7^{\circ}$ , $9.5^{\circ}$ , $12.6^{\circ}$ were observed in the in-plane XRD pattern for the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ film on LSMO (Figure 2a), which can be indexed as the (100), (200), (210) reflections within the ab plane. In sharp contrast, the out-of-plane XRD of the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ film displays significantly different patterns with mainly a broader diffraction peak at $2\theta=28.0^{\circ}$ that is indexed as (002) reflection. The GIXRD patterns imply that the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ film on LSMO adopt a preferred “face-on” orientation. The combination of the experimental XRD profiles and simulation results of bulk material suggests that Cu ions are connected with hexadentate HHTP ligands in $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ , forming 2D honeycomb layers and further stacking perpendicular to the substrate in a slipped-parallel AB mode (Figures 1c, S6 and S7).[25] According to Scherrer’s equation, the average crystalline domain size is estimated as ${\sim}16$ nm from the full-width at half-maximum (FWHM) of the (100) reflection, which is comparable to earlier works.[22b,26] Although slight peak broadening is observed, ${\mathsf{C u}}_{3}{\mathsf{H H H T P}}_{2}$ film was highly crystalline and phase-pure. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images further corroborate the crystalline phase and hexagonal microporous structure of the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ film (Figure S5). The intrinsic porosity of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ was also proved by ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption measurement (Figure S8).
The chemical composition and valency state of Cu ion of the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ film was analyzed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Figure S9). The survey spectrum of XPS confirms the presence of Cu, O, and C elements. The high
resolution XPS spectrum of Cu $2{p}_{3/2}$ shows an asymmetric peak, which could be deconvoluted to a prominent peak at $934.8~\mathsf{e V}$ with satellite peaks at lower binding energies of $932.9~\mathsf{e V}$ , attributed to the $+2$ and $+1$ oxidation state on Cu center, respectively.[14] Thus, the Cu centers exhibit a mixed valency of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{\parallel}$ and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{\iota}$ , while the ligands HHTP exist in both semiquinonate and catecholate state to balance the charges.[10d]
The current-voltage $(I{-}V)$ curve of vertical device based on a 100 nm-thick $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ film was measured by two-probe method (Figures 3a and S10). The resistance magnitude of the random measuring points is near megaohm (Table S2). Moreover, a four-probe technique was used to evaluate the in-plane conductivity of the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ thin film (100 nm-thick) (Figure 3b and S11). The linear and zero drift of $\boldsymbol{I-V}$ curves indicate good Ohmic contact of the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ thin film and Au electrodes (Figure 3b). Figure 3c shows a non-linear increase of the electrical conductivity from 80 to $300\mathsf{K},$ confirming a typical semiconducting nature. The conductivity at $300\mathsf{K}$ was estimated to be $0.29\mathsf{S}$ cm1 in vacuum. The plot ${\tt o f}$ natural logarithm of conductivity (lnσ) versus reciprocal temperature $(1/\overline{{T}})$ reveals a nonlinear relationship (Figure 3d). The data fit well with Arrhenius law over higher temperature $T>240~\mathsf{K})$ , suggesting a dominant thermal activation process. The active energy $(E_{\mathsf{a}})$ was calculated as 0.13 eV based on the slope of lnσ versus $1/\tau$ plot. On the other hand, over the temperature range from 125 to $200~\mathsf{K}.$ , the plot of lnσ versus $T^{-1/4}$ is coincident with the Mott variable range hoping (Mott–VRH) model in the in-plane-structured device (Figure 3d, inset).
To investigate the spin injection and transport in the semiconducting $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ thin films, OSVs with vertical architecture of $\mathsf{L S M O/C u_{3}(H H T P)_{2}/C o/A u}$ were fabricated (Figures 4a and 4b), where Co was used as the top FM electrode and covered by a thin layer of $\mathsf{A u}$ through thermal evaporation. The thicknesses of the LSMO and $\scriptstyle{\dot{\mathsf{C o}}}$ electrodes were around 50 nm, while the thickness of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ film was varied from 30 to $100~\mathsf{n m}$ . To avoid the short circuit and minimize the effect of Co penetration,[27] the OSV device based on thicker $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ film (i.e. $100\mathsf{n m}$ ) was selected for systematical investigations (Figure 4b). The interfacial structure between the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ and Co layers were further investigated by the TEM-EDX and XPS (Figures S12 and S13). In order to assess the MR effect in our OSVs, the magnetic hysteresis $(M-H)$ curves for LSMO/2D $c-$ MOF/Co device and individual LSMO electrode at $10~\mathsf{K}$ was compared, as shown in Figures 4c and S14. Two significant coercivities $(H_{\mathrm{C}})$ were observed in the magnetization curve of the LSMO/2D $c$ -MOF/Co device, indicating that the relative magnetization of the two FM electrodes can be switched between parallel (P) and antiparallel (AP) alignments with the external magnetic field. Moreover, the relatively small coercive field in LSMO/2D $c$ -MOF/Co is around $60\textcircled{>}\textcircled{>}\textcircled{>}$ at $10\mathsf{K}.$ which is consistent with the coercivity of the individual LSMO layer (Figure S14a). Moreover, the $M-H$ curve of the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ film presents nonferromagnetic behavior at $100~\mathsf{K}$ (Figure S15). Thus, the relatively high coercivity (180 Oe at 10 K) in the LSMO/2D cMOF/Co is originated from the Co FM layer. These magnetization characteristics in the 2D $c$ -MOF based OSVs are similar to the previous reported OSVs such as LSMO/P3MT/Co[28] and $\mathsf{L S M O/A l q_{3}/C O}$ .[7]
Figure 4d showcases a typical inverse MR effect of the 2D cMOF based OSVs. The MR is defined as $\mathsf{M R}=(R_{\mathsf{A P}}-R_{\mathsf{P}})/R_{\mathsf{P}}\ \times$ $100\%$ , where $R_{\mathsf{A P}}$ and $R_{\mathsf{P}}$ denote the resistance $R$ measured at anti-parallel and parallel magnetization states, respectively.[27] The MR was measured under the current of $0.1~\upmu\mathsf{A}$ with the magnetic field applied parallel to the plane of the FM electrodes. A large negative MR around $25\%$ was observed at $10~\mathsf{K}$ (Figure 4d). The MR exhibits strong temperature dependence (Figure 4e and Figure S16). For example, the negative MR magnitude of the OSVs based on the $100\mathsf{n m}$ -thick film is around $25\%$ below $50~\mathsf{K},$ and steeply decreased at elevated temperatures higher than 50 K, which is correlated to the damping of the interfacial spin polarization of LSMO at elevated temperatures.[29] Interestingly, a significant negative MR effect could be retained at a temperature as high as $200\mathsf{K}\left(3\%\right)$ , but vanished at $250\mathsf{K}.$ It is well accepted that the spin polarization at the FM electrode surface vanishes at the Curie temperature $(\overline{{T_{C}}})$ . This observation coincides with the experimental $\tau_{c}$ of the LSMO electrode (Figure S14b). Moreover, all the OSVs with different $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ film thickness $(30-100\mathsf{n m})$ exhibit distinctive MR effect (Figure S17). These results support the occurrence of spin injection from LSMO into the  2D $c$ -MOF of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ . The repeatability of the $100\ \mathsf{n m}$ -thick $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ MOF-based OSVs was also tested (Figures S18 and S19). More than $70\%$ of the 2D $\scriptstyle{c}$ -MOF based spin valves show MR valve ranging from $13\%$ to $25\%$ , indicating the high reproducibility of these vertical structured devices.
A schematic energy level diagram of the $\mathsf{L S M O/C u_{3}(H H T P)_{2}/C o}$ device is shown in Figure S20. It is reported that the FM electrodes LSMO and Co exhibit similar work function of $^{-4.8}$ and $-4.9\mathsf{e V}.^{[2]}$ The HOMO energy level of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ calculated from the ultraviolet photoemission valance band spectrum (UPS) was $-5.32\theta\mathsf{V}$ (Figure S21a). The LUMO energy was calculated as $-4.74\mathrm{~eV}$ based on the experimental optical band gap $(\mathsf{E}\mathsf{g}=0.58$ eV, Figures S21b and S21c) which is comparable to the reported value $(0.48\mathsf{e V})$ .[24,30] The energy levels of ${\mathsf{C u}}3({\mathsf{H H T P}})2$ match well with the work function of the two FM electrodes, which facilitate the injection and extraction of charge carriers between the 2D c-MOF and the FM electrodes. The mixed valency of the Cu ions in the framework facilitates charge delocalization and enhanced p-d orbital coupling.[31] Strong electron delocalization among the 2D layers and π-d conjugation between metal ions and ligands benefits the intra-layer charge transport within $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ film.
The temperature dependent $1-V$ curves of the OSVs ( $100~\mathsf{n m}$ thick) present a nonlinear characteristic (Figure S22). Notably, the $1-V$ curves and the dI-dV curves (Figure S23) exhibit a weak temperature dependence and the differential conductance displays parabolic characteristic, indicating the tunnelingdominated transport model in our devices.[6,32] Moreover, the resistivity of the OSVs decreases with decreasing temperature. Based on the previous investigations, the temperature dependent resistivity and the negative MR in our OSVs can be attributed to the Co penetration into the active layer, which has also been proved by TEM and XPS measurement (Figures S12, S13).[27] Furthermore, considering the root-mean-square (RMS) surface roughness of about $4.12\mathsf{n m}$ in our crystalline $100\mathrm{-}\mathsf{n m}\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ film (Figure S4), the effective active barrier height is lower than $100~\mathsf{n m}$ in the devices. Therefore, we can further infer that the spin dependent transport mechanism can be understood as a multistep-tunneling-dominated behavior.
In summary, 2D $c$ -MOF-based OSVs were demonstrated for the first time with a vertical device structure of ${\mathrm{.}}{\mathsf{S M O/C u_{3}(H H T P)_{2}/C o}}$ Layer-by-layer assembly method was used for in-situ growth of organic spacer on the LSMO electrode with controllable thickness. The MR effect can be realized in the OSVs with the thickness of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ thin film varied from 30 to $100~\mathsf{n m}$ . A large negative magnetoresistance with $|\mathsf{M R}|=25\%$ was achieved at $10\mathsf{K}$ These observations indicate a probable spin-tunneling transport process in such materials, although the exact spin-polarized transport physics remains elusive. Our findings indicate that developing proper 2D π-d conjugated MOFs by appropriate chemical design is highly relevant to modulate the electronic structure and spin transport pathway. This work demonstrates that 2D c-MOF could serve as a promising platform for OSVs, which would prompt further physical studies on these novel 2D $c$ -MOF based spintronics. Further improvement of the quality of the 2D c-MOF film to reduce defects and better control of the interface are current in progress in our laboratory.
